his  person's hair which is now positively  charged if this was two strands of her  hair we could see that they would repel  each other and try and get away from  each other as best they can this is why  after you have statically charged her  hair it will stand up like this because  each hair is trying to repel the other  one where is repelling the other one so  that is how static electricity works in  this particular case let's see another  example so let's imagine I've got my  negatively charged balloon negatively  charged remember because it's had loads  of electrons transferred into it because  we've rubbed these two insulating  materials together and I start to bring  this balloon close to this wall so the  wall like the wall is made of matter so  it contains protons neutrons and  electrons because it contains atoms it's  made up of atoms so what happens is as  we start to bring the balloon closer to  the wall these electrons in the surface  of the wall are repelled because we've  got the negative electrons in this  balloon this negatively charged balloons  is going to repel these electrons this  means because all these electrons have  now left the surface of the wall the  wall now is positively charged this  means that if we bring this balloon even  closer to the wall it is in fact going  to stick to the wall because we have the  positively charged wall  being attracted to the negatively  charged balloon so that's how we can  actually induce a charge okay the next  thing we need to know about is earthing  so sometimes now apologies my picture of  a car this is a not going to be the  greatest car ever so sometimes you can  your car can get charged can get subtly  charged so it could be the robbing of  your clothes against the seats that kind  of thing and so as a result your car  could get a it could get a certain  charge now this could potentially be  dangerous because if you went to refuel  your car there could be a sudden  discharge of this of this static ch